\title{Cellular Automata as Algebraic Geometry:\\Boolean Polynomial Models and the Limits of Fixed-Point Complexity}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{July 18, 2026}
\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
Elementary cellular automata are synchronous dynamical systems on binary arrays whose local update depends on three cells. We give a self-contained algebraic description and assess whether the size or dimension of a fixed-point variety measures dynamical complexity. There are exactly $256$ local rules, and each has a unique algebraic normal form over $\mathbb F_2$ of degree at most three. For arbitrary finite neighbor maps, the global update is a polynomial self-map. Rule $0$ has one fixed state, Rule $204$ fixes all $2^n$ states, and Rule $110$ has a nonempty but nonmaximal fixed set on every nonempty finite array. If the right-neighbor map has one forward orbit, Rule $110$ has exactly one fixed state. These results separate fixed-point count, Krull dimension, and computational complexity, and motivate temporal algebraic invariants.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}
An elementary cellular automaton (ECA) evolves a binary row in discrete time. Every site simultaneously reads its left neighbor, itself, and its right neighbor, then applies one truth table. This tiny local mechanism supports behavior ranging from extinction to universal computation. Identifying bits with the field $\mathbb F_2$ turns every truth table into a polynomial and every finite ECA into a polynomial dynamical system.

Fixed configurations solve $F(s)=s$, suggesting an algebraic fixed-point locus. It is tempting to expect complicated rules to have larger loci. Exact examples overturn this expectation: the dynamically inert Rule $204$ fixes everything, while the computationally rich Rule $110$ can have a unique equilibrium. The polynomial framework remains useful, but equilibrium geometry alone is not a complexity scale.

\section{Finite automata and polynomial form}
\begin{definition}
For $n\geq0$, let $I_n=\{0,\ldots,n-1\}$ and $X_n=\{0,1\}^{I_n}$. A local rule is a function $f:\{0,1\}^3\to\{0,1\}$. Given neighbor maps $L,R:I_n\to I_n$, its synchronous global update is
\[
(F_f(s))_i=f(s_{L(i)},s_i,s_{R(i)}).
\]
A configuration is fixed when $F_f(s)=s$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Enumeration of elementary rules]
There are exactly $256$ elementary local rules.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
There are eight binary triples and two independent output choices at each, hence $2^8=256$ functions.
\end{proof}

Write addition and multiplication in $\mathbb F_2$ as exclusive-or and conjunction. A general multilinear ternary polynomial is
\[
P(l,c,r)=a_0+a_1l+a_2c+a_3lc+a_4r+a_5lr+a_6cr+a_7lcr.
\]

\begin{theorem}[Algebraic Normal Form]
For every $f:\mathbb F_2^3\to\mathbb F_2$, there is a unique vector $(a_0,\ldots,a_7)\in\mathbb F_2^8$ for which $f(l,c,r)=P(l,c,r)$ on all Boolean inputs. Thus every elementary rule has degree at most three.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Evaluation at $000$ determines $a_0$. Evaluations at $100,010,001$ then determine the three linear coefficients. The points $110,101,011$ determine the quadratic coefficients after known contributions are removed, and $111$ determines the cubic coefficient. This triangular recovery proves existence and uniqueness. Equivalently, it is M\"obius inversion on the Boolean cube.
\end{proof}

Consequently every coordinate $(F_f(s))_i=P(s_{L(i)},s_i,s_{R(i)})$ is polynomial of degree at most three.

\section{Fixed-point equations and dimension}
The fixed states are the $\mathbb F_2$-solutions of
\[
P(s_{L(i)},s_i,s_{R(i)})-s_i=0 \quad(i\in I_n).
\]
To specify binary states algebraically one also imposes $s_i^2-s_i=0$. Thus the natural fixed-point ideal is
\[
J_f=\langle P(s_{L(i)},s_i,s_{R(i)})-s_i,\ s_i^2-s_i:i\in I_n\rangle
\]
in $\mathbb F_2[s_0,\ldots,s_{n-1}]$. Its rational zero set is precisely the fixed set.

The quotient by the Boolean relations is spanned by squarefree monomials and is finite-dimensional over $\mathbb F_2$. It is therefore Artinian and has Krull dimension zero whenever nonzero. A singleton and $2^n$ isolated Boolean points differ in cardinality, not Krull dimension. Any dimension claim must therefore specify a different ring or an asymptotic invariant.

\section{Exact fixed-point results}
\subsection{Rules 0 and 204}
Rule $0$ is $f_0(l,c,r)=0$.
\begin{theorem}[Rule 0]
For every $n$ and all neighbor maps, Rule $0$ has exactly one fixed configuration, the all-zero state.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Every state updates to zero. Hence a fixed state must be zero, and zero is fixed.
\end{proof}

Rule $204$ is $f_{204}(l,c,r)=c$.
\begin{theorem}[Rule 204]
For every $n$ and all neighbor maps, Rule $204$ fixes every configuration. Its fixed-point count is $2^n$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
At each site $(F_{f_{204}}(s))_i=s_i$, so the global update is the identity on a set of size $2^n$.
\end{proof}

\subsection{Rule 110}
In neighborhood order $000,001,010,011,100,101,110,111$, Rule $110$ has outputs
\[
0,1,1,1,0,1,1,0.
\]
\begin{proposition}[Universal bounds]
On every nonempty finite array, independently of neighbor maps,
\[
0<|\operatorname{Fix}(F_{110})|<2^n.
\]
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
The all-zero state is fixed because $000$ outputs zero. The all-one state is not fixed because $111$ outputs zero. Thus the fixed set is nonempty and omits at least one state.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Zero propagation]
If $s$ is fixed by Rule $110$ and $s_i=0$, then $s_{R(i)}=0$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Among neighborhoods whose center is zero, $001$ and $101$ output one, whereas $000$ and $100$ output zero. Since fixedness requires output zero at $i$, the right input must be zero.
\end{proof}

\begin{definition}
The right-neighbor map is forward transitive if, for every $i,j\in I_n$, there is $k\geq0$ such that $R^k(i)=j$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Rule 110 Singleton Fixed-Point Theorem]
If $n>0$ and $R$ is forward transitive, then Rule $110$ has exactly one fixed configuration, the all-zero state.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Let $s$ be fixed. It is not all one, so some $s_i=0$. The propagation lemma gives $s_{R^k(i)}=0$ for every $k$. Forward transitivity reaches every site, so $s$ is all zero. The zero state is fixed.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Cyclic boundary]
On every nonempty cyclic array with the standard right shift, Rule $110$ has exactly one fixed state.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
Repeated right shifts visit every site, so the preceding theorem applies.
\end{proof}

\section{Algorithms and numerical illustrations}
A rule number $w\in\{0,\ldots,255\}$ is decoded by $b_j=\lfloor w/2^j\rfloor\bmod2$. To obtain algebraic-normal-form coefficients, initialize $a$ with these eight outputs. For each of the three input bits and every mask containing it, replace $a_m$ by $a_m+a_{m\setminus\{q\}}$. This fast M\"obius transform uses $O(k2^k)$ time and $O(2^k)$ space for $k$ inputs.

Fixed points can be enumerated by decoding each integer from $0$ to $2^n-1$ as a state, applying the synchronous update, and comparing output with input. This costs $O(n2^n)$ time and $O(n)$ auxiliary space when only a count is retained. Symbolically, one may instead construct $J_f$ and use Boolean equation solving or elimination.

For cyclic arrays the exact counts are:
\[
\begin{array}{c@{\quad}ccc}
\toprule
n&\text{Rule 0}&\text{Rule 110}&\text{Rule 204}\\
\midrule
1&1&1&2\\2&1&1&4\\3&1&1&8\\4&1&1&16\\8&1&1&256\\12&1&1&4096\\
\bottomrule
\end{array}
\]
These values follow from the theorems, while exhaustive enumeration provides a transparent numerical demonstration.

\section{Interpretation and applications}
Rule $204$ disproves any assertion that maximal fixed-point count signals maximal dynamical complexity: it has no evolution. Rule $110$ demonstrates the converse mismatch: universal computation can coexist with a singleton equilibrium set. Universality concerns encoded initial conditions, propagation, collisions, and arbitrarily long time, not merely equilibrium.

Polynomial encodings nevertheless support useful applications. They allow stable motifs to be classified by Boolean equation solving, hidden variables to be eliminated, and local implications to be propagated through network reachability. Periodic behavior is encoded by $F_f^p(s)=s$. These equations include periods dividing $p$; exact-period points are obtained by excluding solutions belonging to proper divisors.

A local-to-global construction may also organize compatible patterns, but it must specify its regions or topology, assigned objects, restriction maps, and the theorem connecting global sections with configurations. Without these data, richness of sections is not a defined complexity invariant.

\section{Future directions}
Natural next steps are to express periodic shifts explicitly; realize global updates in multivariate polynomial rings over $\mathbb F_2$; and study coordinate rings separately from rational-point counts. Numeric rule decoding should be treated as a bijection with the $256$ truth tables. Exhaustive comparisons across rules must declare boundary conventions, size ranges, statistics, and classification data.

The most important extension is from fixed points to periodic and transient behavior. Since universality concerns unbounded spacetime evolution, growth rates of periodic points, orbit languages, simulation embeddings, and polynomial models of spacetime diagrams are more plausible invariants. Any sheaf construction should likewise be given explicitly before its global sections are compared across rules.

\section{Conclusion}
Every elementary cellular automaton is a degree-at-most-three Boolean polynomial system, and its finite fixed states solve explicit algebraic equations. Yet fixed-point abundance is not computational complexity: Rule $0$ has one equilibrium, Rule $204$ has all $2^n$, and Rule $110$ has one under a forward-transitive right shift. The productive conclusion is to preserve the algebraic bridge, distinguish cardinality from dimension, and use temporal invariants for temporal phenomena.

\end{document}

